"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_010_thumb.jpg", "page": "14", "label": "10", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/14"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_011_thumb.jpg", "page": "15", "label": "11", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/15"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_012_thumb.jpg", "page": "16", "label": "12", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/16"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_013_thumb.jpg", "page": "17", "label": "13", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/17"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_014_thumb.jpg", "page": "18", "label": "14", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/18"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_015_thumb.jpg", "page": "19", "label": "15", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/19"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_016_thumb.jpg", "page": "20", "label": "16", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/20"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_017_thumb.jpg", "page": "21", "label": "17", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/21"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_018_thumb.jpg", "page": "22", "label": "18", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/22"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_019_thumb.jpg", "page": "23", "label": "[19]", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/23"}, {"image": "/11909/11909_ms16800_1_14_020_thumb.jpg", "page": "24", "label": "[20]", "href": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/24"}], "tableOfContents": [], "summary": {"uri": "ordinance-14-november-1842", "series": "JSPPA", "urlTitleValue": "JSPPA6", "documentSeriesIdCompiled": "jsppa6-sub-nccr-sub02-421114", "documentSeriesUri": "/the-papers/administrative-records/jsppa6/jsppa6-sub-nccr/jsppa6-sub-nccr-sub02/jsppa6-sub-nccr-sub02-421114", "documentSeriesTitle": "<p>\r\n\tMeeting, 14 November 1842</p>", "editorialPageNumber": "19", "isPageNumberPresent": false, "searchUrl": "/published/jsp/searchable/papers/54016763/54016763-0023.xml", "clearText": "<html><head></head><body><div class=\"indent wasptag\"><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1699118509206281700\"><a href=\"#1699118509206281700\" class=\"new-scribe-note staticPopup\" title=\"New Scribe\" name=\"trans29\"><img src=\"//cdn.churchofjesuschrist.org/ch/jsp/images/icn-new-scribe-16.png\" alt=\"Indicates a change in handwriting to a different scribe.\"></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Unidentified handwriting ends; <a href=\"/person/james-sloan\" class=\"referenceInPop\">James Sloan</a> resumes.</p><hr></div></div></aside>Sec. 18. The Municipal Court upon issuing a Writ of <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"2974847071775704696\"><a href=\"#2974847071775704696\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"x41899\" topic=\"x41899\" title=\"Habeas corpus\">Habeas Corpus</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Habeas corpus\"><p>“Have the body”; a written order from a court of competent jurisdiction commanding anyone having a person in custody to produce such person at a certain time and place and to state the reasons why he or she is being held in custody. The court will determine...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/topic/habeas-corpus\">View Glossary</a></div></div></aside>, may appoint any suitable Person to serve the same, other than the Marshal, and shall endorse the appointment on the back of said Writ.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"8647350507655105973\"><a href=\"#8647350507655105973\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans35\"><span id=\"documenttrans35\">35</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Section 18 is the only complete section of this ordinance that did not originate with the 1827 state statute on <a href=\"/topic/habeas-corpus\" class=\"referenceInPop\">habeas corpus</a>.</p><hr></div></div></aside></div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">Sec. 19. This Ordinance to take effect and be in force from and after its passage, any Act heretofore to the contrary thereof in any wise notwithstanding.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"2177980132805529163\"><a href=\"#2177980132805529163\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans36\"><span id=\"documenttrans36\">36</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">In addition to stating when the statute was to take effect, the final section of the 1827 statute dealt with how writs of <a href=\"/topic/habeas-corpus\" class=\"referenceInPop\">habeas corpus</a> should be handled when the court was in <a href=\"/topic/vacation\" class=\"referenceInPop\">vacation</a> or when prisoners or witnesses needed to be moved across county lines. The <a href=\"/place/nauvoo-illinois\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Nauvoo</a> City Council dealt with the first issue in their revisions to section 1 of the city ordinance, and the other issue did not apply because of the city council’s limited jurisdiction. (An Act . . . on Writs of Habeas Corpus, pp. 327–328, sec. 20.)</p><hr><div class=\"works-cited-links\"><a href=\"/works-cited\" class=\"pdf\" id=\"comp-works\">Comprehensive Works Cited</a></div><div class=\"source-note\"><p id=\"source-note1\"><span class=\"title\">The Revised Code of Laws, of Illinois, Enacted at the Fifth General Assembly, at Their Session Held at Vandalia, Commencing on the Fourth Day of December, 1826, and Ending the Nineteenth of February, 1827.</span> Vandalia, IL: Robert Blackwell, 1827.</p></div></div></div></aside> passed Nov<span class=\"underscore\">r</span>. 14<span class=\"underscore\">th</span>. 1842.</div> <div class=\"right wasptag\"><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13792117504805605359\"><a href=\"#13792117504805605359\" class=\"new-scribe-note staticPopup\" title=\"New Scribe\" name=\"trans30\"><img src=\"//cdn.churchofjesuschrist.org/ch/jsp/images/icn-new-scribe-16.png\" alt=\"Indicates a change in handwriting to a different scribe.\"></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Signature of JS.</p><hr></div></div></aside><span class=\"josephswriting\">Joseph Smith Mayor</span></div><span class=\"josephswriting\"> </span><div class=\"noindent wasptag\"><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"18423699345837088673\"><a href=\"#18423699345837088673\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"per45\" person=\"per45\" title=\"James Sloan\">James Sloan</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Sloan, James\"><p>28 Oct. 1792–24 Oct. 1886. City recorder, notary public, attorney, judge, farmer. Born in Donaghmore, Co. Tyrone, Ireland. Son of Alexander Sloan and Anne. Married Mary Magill. Baptized into Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. Ordained an elder, ...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/person/james-sloan\">View Full Bio</a></div></div></aside>, Recorder. [<span class=\"italic note-data editorial-comment\" isnote=\"Y\">1/2 page blank</span>] [p. [19]]</div></body></html>", "expandedText": "<html><head></head><body><div class=\"indent wasptag\"><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9697792717629199318\"><a href=\"#9697792717629199318\" class=\"new-scribe-note staticPopup\" title=\"New Scribe\" name=\"trans29\"><img src=\"//cdn.churchofjesuschrist.org/ch/jsp/images/icn-new-scribe-16.png\" alt=\"Indicates a change in handwriting to a different scribe.\"></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Unidentified handwriting ends; <a href=\"/person/james-sloan\" class=\"referenceInPop\">James Sloan</a> resumes.</p><hr></div></div></aside>Sec. 18. The Municipal Court &lt;​upon​&gt; issuing <span class=\"deleted\">the</span> a Writ <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>of <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"17703954548845739092\"><a href=\"#17703954548845739092\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"x41899\" topic=\"x41899\" title=\"Habeas corpus\">Habeas Corpus</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Habeas corpus\"><p>“Have the body”; a written order from a court of competent jurisdiction commanding anyone having a person in custody to produce such person at a certain time and place and to state the reasons why he or she is being held in custody. The court will determine...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/topic/habeas-corpus\">View Glossary</a></div></div></aside>, may appoint any <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>suitable Person to serve the same, other than the <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Marshal, &lt;​and shall endorse the​&gt; <span class=\"deleted\">which</span> appointment <span class=\"deleted\">shall be endorsed</span> <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>on the back of said Writ.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"8147271937262600361\"><a href=\"#8147271937262600361\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans35\"><span id=\"documenttrans35\">35</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Section 18 is the only complete section of this ordinance that did not originate with the 1827 state statute on <a href=\"/topic/habeas-corpus\" class=\"referenceInPop\">habeas corpus</a>.</p><hr></div></div></aside></div> <div class=\"indent wasptag\">Sec. 19. This Ordinance to take effect and be in force <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>from and after its passage, any Act heretofore <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>to the contrary thereof in any wise notwithstanding.<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"9586145497962045844\"><a href=\"#9586145497962045844\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans36\"><span id=\"documenttrans36\">36</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">In addition to stating when the statute was to take effect, the final section of the 1827 statute dealt with how writs of <a href=\"/topic/habeas-corpus\" class=\"referenceInPop\">habeas corpus</a> should be handled when the court was in <a href=\"/topic/vacation\" class=\"referenceInPop\">vacation</a> or when prisoners or witnesses needed to be moved across county lines. The <a href=\"/place/nauvoo-illinois\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Nauvoo</a> City Council dealt with the first issue in their revisions to section 1 of the city ordinance, and the other issue did not apply because of the city council’s limited jurisdiction. (An Act . . . on Writs of Habeas Corpus, pp. 327–328, sec. 20.)</p><hr><div class=\"works-cited-links\"><a href=\"/works-cited\" class=\"pdf\" id=\"comp-works\">Comprehensive Works Cited</a></div><div class=\"source-note\"><p id=\"source-note1\"><span class=\"title\">The Revised Code of Laws, of Illinois, Enacted at the Fifth General Assembly, at Their Session Held at Vandalia, Commencing on the Fourth Day of December, 1826, and Ending the Nineteenth of February, 1827.</span> Vandalia, IL: Robert Blackwell, 1827.</p></div></div></div></aside> <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>passed Nov<span class=\"superscript\"><span class=\"underscore\">r</span>.</span> 14<span class=\"superscript\"><span class=\"underscore\">th</span>.</span> 1842.</div> <div class=\"right wasptag\"><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"5942953367566093062\"><a href=\"#5942953367566093062\" class=\"new-scribe-note staticPopup\" title=\"New Scribe\" name=\"trans30\"><img src=\"//cdn.churchofjesuschrist.org/ch/jsp/images/icn-new-scribe-16.png\" alt=\"Indicates a change in handwriting to a different scribe.\"></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">Signature of JS.</p><hr></div></div></aside><span class=\"josephswriting\">Joseph Smith Mayor</span></div><span class=\"josephswriting\"> </span><div class=\"noindent wasptag\"><aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"6412227559382621341\"><a href=\"#6412227559382621341\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"per45\" person=\"per45\" title=\"James Sloan\">James Sloan</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Sloan, James\"><p>28 Oct. 1792–24 Oct. 1886. City recorder, notary public, attorney, judge, farmer. Born in Donaghmore, Co. Tyrone, Ireland. Son of Alexander Sloan and Anne. Married Mary Magill. Baptized into Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints. Ordained an elder, ...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/person/james-sloan\">View Full Bio</a></div></div></aside>, Recorder. [<span class=\"italic note-data editorial-comment\" isnote=\"Y\">1/2 page blank</span>] [p. [19]]</div></body></html>", "calendarsOfDocuments": [], "versions": [{"versionTitle": "Ordinance, 14 November 1842", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842/1", "versionFeatured": ""}, {"versionTitle": "Nauvoo City Council Minute Book, 1841–1845", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/nauvoo-city-council-minute-book-1841-1845/125", "versionFeatured": ""}, {"versionTitle": "Ordinance, 14 November 1842, as Published in the <em xmlns:tei=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xmlns:lds=\"https://ldsc.xmlref.com/local/ldschurch\">Wasp</em>", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/ordinance-14-november-1842-as-published-in-the-wasp/1", "versionFeatured": ""}, {"versionTitle": "History, 1838–1856, volume D-1 [1 August 1842–1 July 1843]",